🚀 Day 14 of #DrGViswanathanChallenge

Today, I solved LeetCode 287 – Find the Duplicate Number.

This was one of the most interesting problems I've solved so far because it introduced me to Floyd's Cycle Detection Algorithm (Tortoise and Hare). Instead of using extra memory or modifying the array, I learned how to detect the duplicate by treating the array as a linked list.

💡 Approach: Floyd's Cycle Detection (Tortoise & Hare)

The key observation is that each array value can be treated as the next pointer of a linked list. Since one number is repeated, it naturally forms a cycle.

I solved the problem in two phases:

Cycle Detection: Used a slow pointer (moves one step) and a fast pointer (moves two steps) until they met.
Finding the Cycle Entrance: Reset the slow pointer to the beginning and moved both pointers one step at a time. The point where they met again was the duplicate number.

This elegant approach satisfies all the constraints without modifying the array and uses only constant extra space.

